; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Fibonacci and Its Tree of Calls
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, fibonacci, double recursion, counting, tree
; SUMMARY:  Computes Fibonacci numbers the slow, natural way and draws the tree of calls that the computation makes.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two before it:
;   0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13 21 ... FIB follows that rule directly, so every
;   call makes TWO more calls. The program counts how many calls that
;   adds up to, and then draws the calls for fib 5 as a tree. Each
;   circle is one call, labeled with its input. You can see fib 3 and
;   fib 2 being worked out again and again.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run. Read the printed table, then look at the tree.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        output (fib :n - 1) + (fib :n - 2)
;      Right-click the red dot, choose "Stop only when...", and type
;        :n = 2
;      Run with F5. Look at the Call Stack: how did we get down to 2?
;      Press F5 again. It stops at :n = 2 again, through a different
;      path. Count how many times you land there for fib 6.
;   3. In the Watch panel add  :calls  and see it climb as you continue.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * double recursion (a procedure calling itself twice)
;   * why the obvious recursive answer can be very slow
;   * drawing a tree shape that mirrors the calls
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change the table to go up to 22. Notice the pause near the end.
;   * Try drawing the tree for 6 (change calltree 5 to calltree 6 and
;     make the circles smaller).
;   * See fibonacci-remembered.lgo for a way to make it fast.
; ============================================================

make "calls 0

to fib :n
  make "calls :calls + 1
  if :n < 2 [output :n]
  output (fib :n - 1) + (fib :n - 2)
end

to table :upto
  for [i 1 :upto] [
    make "calls 0
    localmake "f fib :i
    print (sentence "fib :i "= :f [needed] :calls [calls])
  ]
end

; Draw the call tree. The turtle sits on the circle for call :n.
; Its two children go below, spread apart by :spread steps. The
; spread halves at each level so the branches do not overlap.
to calltree :n :spread
  if :n < 2 [node :n stop]
  child :n - 1 (minus :spread) :spread
  child :n - 2 :spread :spread
  node :n
end

; Walk to a child position, draw its subtree, and walk back, so the
; turtle is in the same place it started. That "come back home" habit
; is what makes recursive drawings work.
to child :k :dx :spread
  localmake "here pos
  localmake "target (list xcor + :dx ycor - 85)
  ; draw the line only BETWEEN the two circles, not across them
  setpencolor 15
  setheading towards :target
  localmake "d distance :target
  penup fd 11 pendown fd :d - 22 penup fd 11 pendown
  setheading 0
  calltree :k :spread / 2
  penup setpos :here pendown
end

to node :n
  ; base cases are green, calls that recurse are orange
  ifelse :n < 2 [setpencolor 10] [setpencolor 14]
  ; walk round a circle of radius 11 from its bottom point
  penup bk 11 pendown
  filled pencolor [lt 90 repeat 36 [fd 1.92 rt 10] rt 90]
  ; nudge left and down so the number sits in the middle
  penup fd 11 lt 90 fd 4 rt 90 bk 6 pendown
  setpencolor 0
  label :n
  penup fd 6 rt 90 fd 4 lt 90 pendown
end

print [Fibonacci numbers and how many calls they cost]
table 18
clearscreen hideturtle
setlabelheight 14
penup setxy 40 200 pendown
calltree 5 200
penup setxy -190 -240 setpencolor 0
label [Each circle is one call to FIB. Green ones stop at once.]
